Template for projects/<project>/<harness>/Statements.v.  Prose blocks are one
paragraph per line; `oft generate` wraps them (render.wrap).  Every value
comes from harness.yaml; the Rocq terms come from tools/oft/shapes.py.

@@ file
(** * $project / $harness: theorem statements

$header *)

From Stdlib Require Import QArith.
From OssFuzzTheorems.Core Require Import Shapes.
From $rocq Require Images$distribution_require.
$sections
(** ** Proving a statement

$proving *)

@@ header
SUBJECT.  $subject, static riscv64 Linux programs run as [<file> FILE]:
$build_table
[Images] names their memory images.  $driver

WHAT THE HARNESS CHECKS.  $checks

Whether each statement is open, proved or known false is recorded in the harness README, not here: this file changes only when a statement does.

@@ subject_oss-fuzz
$count build$s of OSS-Fuzz's [$harness] harness for $library

@@ subject_upstream
$count build$s of $project's own fuzz target [$harness], as OSS-Fuzz builds it, for $library

@@ subject_upstream_no_oss_fuzz
$count build$s of $project's own fuzz target [$harness], which OSS-Fuzz does not run, for $library

@@ subject_this-repository
$count build$s of the API driver written for this collection, over $library

@@ subject_oss-verify
$count build$s of the fuzz target [$harness], which OSS Verify builds and OSS-Fuzz does not run, for $library

@@ build_plain
  plain       no sanitizer
@@ build_ubsan
  ubsan       UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer; a failed check executes a trap
@@ build_ubsan_rust
  ubsan       rustc's UB checks (status 79); UBSan in C code (it traps)
@@ build_asan
  asan        AddressSanitizer; a report aborts
@@ build_asan-ubsan
  asan_ubsan  both

@@ driver_harness
A driver linked into every build reads FILE as [u16 config word][config bytes][u32 allocation mask][payload] (little-endian; the low 15 bits of the config word are the config length, and this harness binary ignores the mask), calls $entry(payload, length) one to four times ((config[0] & 3) + 1; once when there is no config), and prints each return value.  With a wrong argument count, a missing or unreadable FILE, a FILE shorter than its header, or one of 64 MiB or more, it exits with status 2 without calling the harness.  Every statement quantifies over all of these cases and over every other process input (see Vocabulary in OssFuzzTheorems.Core.Shapes).

@@ driver_api
A driver linked into every build reads FILE as [u16 config word][config bytes][u32 allocation mask][payload] (little-endian; the low 15 bits of the config word are the config length) and runs the payload as a program of calls into $library_name's API ($source); a set bit of the allocation mask makes the corresponding allocation fail.  With a wrong argument count, a missing or unreadable FILE, a FILE shorter than its header, or one of 64 MiB or more, it exits with status 2 without running anything.  Every statement quantifies over all of these cases and over every other process input (see Vocabulary in OssFuzzTheorems.Core.Shapes).

@@ driver_rust
  In this Rust fuzz target, the OSS Verify port's hooks end a failed allocation with status 2 and a panic (a failed assertion, an unwrap, an overflow check or a failed rustc UB check) with status 79, each after one line on standard error: neither aborts.

@@ driver_python
  In this Python fuzz target, the harness and the Python code it imports are bytecode frozen into the binary and run by a static CPython 3.11.13, which the driver starts after it reads FILE, before the first call.  The OSS Verify port's host ends an uncaught Python exception (a failed check of the harness included) with status 79, an uncaught MemoryError with status 2 and an uncaught SystemExit with status 77, and a failure while it starts the interpreter and runs the harness module with status 80 (2 when that failure is an uncaught MemoryError), each after one line on standard error: none of them aborts.  CPython's own fatal errors still abort, for example when an allocation it makes while it starts is refused.

@@ driver_harness_go
A driver written in Go and linked into the build reads FILE as [u16 config word][config bytes][u32 allocation mask][payload] (little-endian; the low 15 bits of the config word are the config length, and the mask is ignored) and calls the Go fuzz target $entry on the payload one to four times ((config[0] & 3) + 1; once when there is no config), as OSS-Fuzz's Go helpers call it, and prints one line per call: a go-fuzz Fuzz(data []byte) int function gets the payload, and its return value is printed and does not change the exit status; for a testing.F fuzz test, the function its f.Fuzz call registers gets arguments that OSS-Fuzz's testing shim decodes from the payload (go-fuzz-headers: a []byte argument is a slice whose length the payload's first four bytes choose), it is not called when the payload is too short to decode them, a t.Skip ends the call normally, and 0 is printed.  With a wrong argument count, a missing or unreadable FILE, a FILE shorter than its header, or one of 64 MiB or more, it exits with status 2 without calling the harness.  Every statement quantifies over all of these cases and over every other process input (see Vocabulary in OssFuzzTheorems.Core.Shapes).

@@ driver_go
  In this Go fuzz target, all of the code is built by Go $go's compiler, assembler and linker, with no cgo and no C library: the harness, the library, Go's standard library, the driver and the Go runtime with the OSS Verify port's patches (no signal handlers, one thread, no reads of the environment, /proc or /sys).  A Go fuzz target ends a failure with an exit status, never with abort(): a panic on the harness's goroutine (a runtime error included) and t.Fatal, t.Error or t.Fail in a fuzz test exit with status 79, os.Exit during a call of the harness with status 77, and any other fatal runtime error that is not out of memory (a panic on another goroutine, a deadlock, a stack overflow, a concurrent map write) with status 80; the runtime exits with status 2 when the system refuses it memory for its heap, a stack or its own data; and a load through a nil pointer is a segmentation fault (status 139), because the patched runtime installs no signal handler.  Go builds have no sanitizer, so the only statement is a halts statement about the plain build, which allows only the statuses 0 and 2.

@@ checks_some
$summary  The $who checks:
$items
